# Every text file checks out with LF, on every machine.
#
# This is not a style preference. A C# raw string literal contains the newlines of the file it is
# written in, verbatim, so a test fixture written as """...""" is LF or CRLF according to how git
# handed the .cs file to the compiler -- and a test that computes an offset from a "\n" marker then
# finds a different string than the one it is searching. Without this file that difference is
# decided by each machine's core.autocrlf: this repository's author has it set to `input`, and
# GitHub's Windows runners default it to `true`, so fifteen editor tests passed locally and failed
# on the first run of CI, for a reason that is nowhere in the diff.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content and leave it alone; `eol=lf` is what removes the
# machine from the decision. The repository already stores LF, so nothing in it changes -- what
# changes is that a checkout can no longer disagree with it.
#
# The compiler itself is not line-ending sensitive and does not need this. A line ends at \n, a lone
# \r is an ordinary character, and in \r\n the \r belongs to the line it ends -- LineMap says so and
# SourceSpanTests.ACarriageReturnBelongsToTheLineItEnds pins it against a fixture that is CRLF on
# purpose. That test keeps working here, because an escaped \r\n inside a literal is content rather
# than a line ending.
* text=auto eol=lf
